WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.132
Construction work crosses sites, office teams and
documents. MAYAK gives those handoffs a shared record.

2
00:00:05.172 --> 00:00:09.960
I built the product, its access model and the infrastructure that keeps it running.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.234
Tasks, photos, timesheets, materials, contracts and purchase
requests are organised around construction projects.

4
00:00:15.274 --> 00:00:20.960
Materials use a movement history: receipts, write-offs
and transfers explain the balance instead of replacing it.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.460
A site manager requests plywood and accessories,
with separate items, a required date and an approver.

6
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.960
Approval hands it to procurement. Each material
item needs invoice files before finance can review it.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.732
Procurement keeps supplier alternatives in named
groups, with comments explaining the options.

8
00:00:36.772 --> 00:00:42.960
Finance selects the actual files to use. Unselected
alternatives remain visible in history rather than disappearing.

9
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.733
The SQL routine locks the request, then checks the actor, company and current stage.

10
00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:52.846
It rejects foreign files and incomplete selections:
every material item must have a chosen invoice.

11
00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:57.960
File decisions, request status, the register entry
and the event commit in one database transaction.

12
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.283
Procurement receives the approved request to finish
the order. The dispatcher receives its chosen invoices.

13
00:01:03.323 --> 00:01:08.960
Supply can close its work independently. The register
entry remains visible and turns green when registered.

14
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.778
A previous actor can recall their handoff; revision
can return work to an earlier permitted stage.

15
00:01:14.818 --> 00:01:20.960
A direct-invoice route goes straight to finance and
dispatch. It does not create a supply completion task.

16
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.960
Rank controls managerial authority. Supply, finance and
dispatcher duties can be combined without raising rank.

17
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.960
Company and project rules constrain normal queries
through RLS. Privileged routines perform their own checks.

18
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.182
In the task module, submitting work moves it to review.
The task author accepts it or returns it for revision.

19
00:01:40.222 --> 00:01:45.960
The status selector cannot bypass acceptance. If the
author is deleted, management can resolve the review.

20
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.293
Next.js server actions use the user session to call
self-hosted Supabase through its HTTP gateway.

21
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:56.627
Auth handles sessions; PostgreSQL enforces data rules;
Storage keeps files. Explicit admin paths are separate.

22
00:01:56.667 --> 00:02:01.960
For invoice downloads, the server checks file access
before streaming from a 60-second signed URL.

23
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.960
On phones, photos are resized before upload. Smaller files
reduce bandwidth without requiring a separate mobile app.

24
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.313
MAX pairing uses a one-time code stored as a hash.
Notifications link the next person to the request.

25
00:02:14.353 --> 00:02:19.960
Delivery is best-effort: a messaging failure must
not become the source of truth for the workflow.

26
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.116
Docker and Nginx run the service. Encrypted offsite
snapshots preserve the database, files and configuration.

27
00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:30.960
I tested recovery in a separate directory and database,
including the file attributes needed for correct downloads.

28
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.460
I translated operating rules into workflows, built the
interface and backend, and implemented the access model.

29
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.960
I also handled deployment, server migration, backups
and fixes driven by people using MAYAK at work.
